I was getting letters from Taliban and a  couple times they showed up to our house  they were telling me that they're gonna  kill me are they gonna kill a member of  my family are they gonna kidnap my son  interpreters have become a very big  target of the Taliban and al-qaeda  there's been a lot of beheadings of  people that have worked with the West  and if we completely pull out of  Afghanistan and we don't bring these  interpreters back the ones that were  promised an opportunity for a visa in  the United States they're gonna be  killed  u.s. immigration policy has been accused  of being slow complicated and  inefficient but can it also be accused  of abandoning foreign individuals who  have put their lives on the line in  service to America that's exactly what's  happening right now too many Afghan  individuals who have served with the US  military as interpreters they're often  seen as the most crucial element in a  military unit without interpreters the  military could not communicate easily  with the local population however the  job comes at a high price they're  labeled as traitors by the Taliban and  live under constant fear for their lives  if they find me they will kill me like  my name was added to to the Taliban  killers they found my picture and  everything and they knew even when where  I'm living in Kabul like thousands of  other brave Afghan interpreters Janus  risked his life for his love of America  and for his belief that America would  follow through on its promise in order  to recruit folks that were going to be  very highly targeted by the enemy  translators they're the bridge between  the US military and the local population  so in order to recruit them we did so  with the promise that look you do hard  work for the United States of America  you put your life at risk you'll have an  opportunity to come to the United States  because we know doggone well that  they're gonna be targeted both during  operations and after congressman  Kinzinger is an officer in the Air  National Guard and a veteran of Iraq and  Afghanistan he has led the charge in  Congress to amend and extend the afghan  special immigrant visa program which was  initiated in 2009 as an attempt to keep  america's promise to its allies the  well-intentioned program allocates a  limited number of visas a year to Afghan  Nationals seeking refuge as a result of  their service to the United States  government however the program has been  largely inefficient bogged down by  bureaucracy the program can take years  to process an application in the  meantime many interpreters have been  killed while waiting and I thought it  will take a couple months but it took me  two years this is sometimes the result  of big government from 2009 to 2013  7,500 Afghan visas were authorized by  Congress through this program but only  around 2000 were actually issued by the  State Department the State Department  has  bonded to criticism by ramping up  efforts to improve the processing time  so far 2014 has seen a tremendous  increase in the number of visas issued  approximately 2,300 Afghans who worked  for the u.s. received a visa out of 3000  allocated however this program is set to  expire this fall with around six  thousand more applicants left in the  backlog Kinzinger in the State  Department are trying to get Congress to  extend their program for another year  and to raise the annual visa limit so  that more Afghans will have a chance to  seek shelter in the United States I  think a lot of the rejection and a lot  of the lack of processing comes from  very strict requirements and plus the  fact that this is a bureaucratic  government I mean when you deal with big  bureaucracies sometimes people are more  concerned with not wanting to make a  mistake than they are with sometimes  maybe clicking yes to bring somebody  over here so while we do need to have  good background checks and we have to be  cautious about this it's been way too  slow at this point and a lot of  translators have given their lives in  the wait Janus was able to escape that  fate and he moved to Virginia in October  with his wife and two children largely  due to the efforts of army captain Matt  Zeller  during his time as an interpreter Janus  was credited with saving the lives of  five American servicemembers and Zeller  was one of them rounds have been getting  closer their artillery had been getting  closer and closer with every salvo and  so I knew the next one was probably  laying on top of me I just thought oh  this is it I die here now I looked at my  watch 450 in the afternoons like I die  around 450 in the afternoon in April  28th 2008 and I didn't ever get to say  goodbye to my mom or anybody to have  kids and I just I kind of was starting  to make my peace the next thing I  remember is I remember  returning to return fire at this guy and  I felt this body land next to me and  then I turn and I see Janus I look like  who the hell are you because I'm Janis  I'm one of your translators and then I  look just passed it what I see the two  guys drop dead and he I realize he's  just shot and killed these two guys and  they were directly behind me Janus  literally had my back in 2008 Zeller and  the rest of his unit returned to the  United States while Janus stayed behind  and continued his work isn't  Berger in Afghanistan it was the hardest  goodbye I've ever had my life they're  soldiers just like us I felt like I was  leaving a member of my year behind  Zeller promised Janis that he would  bring him to safety in the United States  he eventually succeeded after five long  years of pressuring the State Department  now the two friends are focused on  bringing other interpreters to America  when we talk about who we want the  United States of America people that  were willing to really stand up and  fight for freedom are the kind of people  that we want here anyway
 

